      "bestPractices": "# LinkedIn best practices\n\nA sourced checklist of current tactics for writing on LinkedIn. Use it as a pre-flight check, not\na recipe — the house voice (first-person, plain, concrete) wins over any single rule.\n\n## The hook\n\n- [ ] The opening earns the \"…see more\" click. Only ~200 characters show on mobile (~300 on\n      desktop) before the feed truncates the post — the first line decides whether anyone reads on.\n- [ ] Lead with a stop-the-scroll move: a surprising stat, a bold or contrarian claim, an open\n      loop (a curiosity gap that only the rest of the post closes), or a one-line mini-story setup.\n- [ ] No throat-clearing, no \"A few thoughts on…\", no \"I'm humbled / thrilled to announce\".\n\n## Length & format\n\n- [ ] One idea per post. A how-to or a list of concrete takeaways tends to land best.\n- [ ] Short lines and single-sentence paragraphs with whitespace between them — it's read on a\n      phone, and white space keeps it scannable.\n- [ ] Concrete over abstract: a number, a result, a before/after, a specific moment.\n- [ ] Deliver the value in the feed. Self-contained text posts are what people most engage with;\n      don't make the reader leave to get the point.\n\n## Close\n\n- [ ] End on a memorable line, a single clear takeaway, or one genuine question.\n- [ ] A real question invites comments — and comments signal value to the algorithm more than\n      likes do. But ask because you want the answer, not as \"Agree? 👇\" bait.\n\n## Links\n\n- [ ] No external link in the post body. An external link in the post can cut reach noticeably,\n      and link-in-comments posts have been measured at roughly 2–3x the impressions of\n      link-in-post ones.\n- [ ] If the piece needs a link, put it in the first comment and say \"link in the comments\".\n\n## Hashtags\n\n- [ ] 3–5 relevant hashtags, no more. Precision beats volume.\n- [ ] Prefer specific/niche tags (`#BusinessWriting`) over broad ones (`#Business`).\n- [ ] Group them at the end of the post to keep the message clean. Hashtags now act as topic/SEO\n      signals, not a discovery feed — they amplify strong copy, they don't rescue weak copy.\n- [ ] CamelCase multi-word tags (`#SocialMediaMarketing`) for readability and screen readers.\n\n## Mentions & format\n\n- [ ] @mention only people likely to respond; keep it to a handful per post.\n- [ ] At most one emoji, and only if it earns its place. No emoji clutter, no engagement-bait\n      reaction polls or \"like if…\" chains — the algorithm suppresses those.\n- [ ] Vary format over time. Document/carousel posts (uploaded PDFs) and short video tend to draw\n      stronger engagement than plain text, but the best format is the one you can sustain with\n      genuinely useful content. See `post-specs.md` for carousel specs.\n\n## Sources\n\n- HubSpot — How Long Should Your Social Media Posts Be: https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/character-count-guide\n- HubSpot — The Most Engaging Topics on LinkedIn: https://blog.hubspot.com/marketing/top-phrases-topics-linkedin-data\n- Sprout Social — LinkedIn Best Practices: https://sproutsocial.com/insights/linkedin-best-practices/\n- Sprout Social — How the LinkedIn Algorithm Works (2026): https://sproutsocial.com/insights/linkedin-algorithm/\n- Sprout Social — LinkedIn Hashtags: How to Use Them in 2026: https://sproutsocial.com/insights/linkedin-hashtags/\n- Sprout Social — Why You Put Links in the Comments on LinkedIn: https://sproutsocial.com/insights/linkedin-link-in-comments/\n- Buffer — How to Create LinkedIn Carousel Posts: https://buffer.com/resources/linkedin-carousels/\n",
      "specs": "# X (Twitter) post specs\n\nQuick-lookup specs for drafting and publishing on X. Default to free-account limits unless the\nwriter says the account has Premium.\n\n## Character limits\n\n| Surface | Limit |\n| --- | --- |\n| Post (free account) | 280 characters |\n| Post (X Premium, any tier) | up to 25,000 characters |\n| In-feed preview of a long post | ~280 characters, then \"Show more\" |\n| Bio | 160 characters |\n| Display name | 50 characters |\n| Username / handle | 4–15 characters |\n| Direct message | 10,000 characters |\n| URL (auto-shortened) | counts as 23 characters, any length |\n\n- Counts include spaces, punctuation, emojis, and hashtags.\n- Free accounts can't create long posts; their text truncates at 280.\n- Practical target: 240–259 characters tends to earn the most engagement (Sprout); keep the\n  full point inside the first ~280 so the in-feed preview alone delivers value.\n\n## Hashtags\n\n- 0–2 real, relevant hashtags. 1–2 can lift engagement; 3+ tends to lower it. No hashtag walls.\n\n## Media\n\n- Native image or video lifts engagement; X favors native (especially video) over off-platform links.\n- Add media only when it carries the point — text-only posts perform strongly on X.\n\n## Links\n\n- External links cut reach (Sprout: ~50–90% reduction). Put a needed link in the first reply and\n  say so, rather than in the main post. Space multiple link posts out over time.\n\n## Cadence\n\n- Sustainable daily posting beats bursts; ~3–5 posts/day suits growth-focused accounts.\n- Weekday mornings (~9–11 AM, Tue–Thu) skew highest on average — but post when your audience is on.\n\n## Sources\n\n- Sprout Social — How long should social posts be? (character counter): https://sproutsocial.com/insights/social-media-character-counter/\n- Sprout Social — How the Twitter (X) Algorithm Works in 2026: https://sproutsocial.com/insights/twitter-algorithm/\n- Buffer — Best Time to Post on Twitter/X in 2026: https://buffer.com/resources/best-time-to-post-on-twitter-x/\n- HubSpot — How to Market on X (formerly Twitter): https://blog.hubspot.com/blog/tabid/6307/bid/25084/the-ultimate-cheat-sheet-for-expert-twitter-marketing.aspx\n"
